  <h2>Federal Minimum Wage</h2>
  <p>
    The federal minimum wage is $17.30 per hour as of April 1, 2025. It applies to employees in federally
    regulated sectors including banks, airlines, railways, telecommunications companies, Canada Post, and
    First Nations band councils. The federal rate is adjusted annually on April 1 based on the Consumer
    Price Index. If the provincial or territorial minimum wage is higher than the federal rate, the higher
    rate applies to federally regulated workers in that jurisdiction.
  </p>

  <h2>Provincial Minimum Wage Details</h2>

  <h3>Ontario Minimum Wage</h3>
  <p>
    Ontario's general minimum wage is $17.20 per hour, effective October 1, 2024. The province reviews its
    minimum wage annually and adjusts it based on the Ontario CPI. Ontario eliminated the lower student
    minimum wage in 2022, so all workers regardless of age earn the same general rate. Liquor servers in
    Ontario now receive the full general minimum wage; the previous lower rate for liquor servers was
    eliminated on January 1, 2022.
  </p>

  <h3>British Columbia Minimum Wage</h3>
  <p>
    BC's minimum wage is $17.40 per hour as of June 1, 2024. BC adjusts its rate annually on June 1 based
    on the average percentage change in CPI. The province also eliminated the lower liquor server wage in
    2021. BC has a separate rate for live-in camp leaders ($116.86 per day) and live-in home support workers
    (half the minimum wage rate for each hour of work or available time).
  </p>

  <h3>Alberta Minimum Wage</h3>
  <p>
    Alberta's minimum wage has remained at $15.00 per hour since October 1, 2018, making it the lowest
    general minimum wage in Canada alongside Saskatchewan. Alberta does not have a legislated schedule for
    future increases. Students under 18 who work 28 hours per week or less earn a youth minimum wage of
    $13.00 per hour. Alberta also eliminated the lower liquor server wage in 2016.
  </p>

  <h3>Quebec Minimum Wage</h3>
  <p>
    Quebec's general minimum wage is $15.75 per hour, effective May 1, 2024. The province adjusts its rate
    annually on May 1. Quebec maintains a separate lower rate for employees who receive tips (tipped
    workers), currently set at $12.60 per hour. This tipped wage is the lowest sub-minimum rate in Canada
    and applies primarily to restaurant servers, bartenders, and delivery workers.
  </p>

  <h2>Tipped and Liquor Server Wages</h2>
  <p>
    Most provinces have eliminated separate lower minimum wage rates for liquor servers and tipped employees.
    However, a few jurisdictions maintain distinct rates:
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Province</th>
          <th>Tipped / Liquor Server Rate</th>
          <th>General Rate</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Quebec</td><td>$12.60/hr (tipped employees)</td><td>$15.75/hr</td></tr>
        <tr><td>All other provinces</td><td>Same as general rate</td><td>Varies</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Ontario, BC, Alberta, Manitoba, and most other provinces have moved to a single minimum wage rate for all
    workers regardless of whether they receive tips. Quebec remains the notable exception with its lower
    tipped employee rate.
  </p>

  <h2>Youth and Student Minimum Wages</h2>
  <p>
    Only a few provinces have youth or student-specific minimum wage rates that differ from the general rate:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Alberta:</strong> $13.00 per hour for students under 18 working 28 hours per week or less.</li>
    <li><strong>Ontario:</strong> Students under 18 who work 28 hours per week or less during the school year
      previously earned a lower rate, but Ontario harmonized student wages to the general minimum in 2022.</li>
    <li><strong>Other provinces:</strong> Most provinces apply the general minimum wage to all workers
      regardless of age.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>History of Minimum Wage Increases</h2>
  <p>
    Minimum wages across Canada have increased significantly over the past decade. In 2015, most provinces
    had minimum wages between $10.20 and $11.25 per hour. Alberta led one of the most dramatic increases,
    going from $10.20 in 2015 to $15.00 by 2018 under the NDP government's plan. Ontario saw major increases
    from $11.00 in 2015 to $14.00 in 2018, followed by a freeze, and then further increases to $17.20 by 2024.
    BC increased its minimum wage from $10.25 in 2015 to $17.40 by 2024 through annual CPI-linked adjustments.
  </p>
  <p>
    The federal minimum wage was reintroduced in 2021 at $15.00 per hour after being dormant since 1996 (when
    it was set to match provincial rates). Since its reintroduction, the federal rate has been adjusted
    annually based on CPI, reaching $17.30 in 2025.
  </p>

